\section{Ordinal and other category responses}{

\code{"response"} (\code{"ordinary"}) and \code{"pearson"} are refused for an
ordinal family (\code{cumulative()}, \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()}, \code{acat()}), a
\code{categorical()} family and a \code{multinomial()} family. brms refuses
the same two types for the same families ("Predictive errors are not
defined for ordinal or categorical models"): the response is a
category or a vector of counts over categories, so \code{y - E[Y]} has no
scale to be read on.

On an ordinal fit, \code{"osa"} gives a residual that uses only the
order. It uses \code{"oneStepGeneric"} over the discrete support \verb{1..K},
which makes the residuals randomized quantile residuals.
\code{\link[=dharma_residuals]{dharma_residuals()}} is the simulation-based alternative.
\code{"deviance"} is refused, as it is for every family without a
standard unit deviance.
}

\section{Deviance residuals}{

\code{"deviance"} returns \code{sign(y - E[Y]) * sqrt(w * d)}, where the unit
deviance \verb{d = 2 * (loglik of the saturated fit - loglik at the fitted value)} is taken with the dispersion parameter held at its estimate,
and \code{w} is the \code{weights()} addition term (1 by default). For the
exponential-dispersion families this is the glm unit deviance, so a
fixed-effect fit reproduces \code{residuals(glm(...), type = "deviance")}
exactly.

Supported families: \code{gaussian}, \code{poisson}, \code{binomial}, \code{bernoulli},
\code{Gamma}, \code{exponential}, \code{inverse.gaussian}, \code{negbinomial}
(\code{nbinom2}), \code{nbinom1}, \code{geometric}, \code{beta}, and \code{tweedie}. Every
other family is refused: ordinal, mixture, multinomial, hurdle,
zero-inflated and location-shift families have no standard unit
deviance. \code{nbinom1} follows glmmTMB and evaluates the
negative-binomial size at the fitted row's \code{mu / phi}; letting the
size follow the saturated mean is not a deviance (the difference goes
negative). \code{trunc()}ed and \code{cens()}ed responses are refused as well,
because the fitted likelihood there is not the family's own density.

A gaussian response with \code{se()} has no common dispersion for a raw
squared residual to be measured against, so the known variance
enters as a glm prior weight \code{sigma^2 / s_i^2} on top of \code{w}, where
\code{s_i} is the row's residual sd (the quantity \code{"pearson"} divides
by). Without \code{se()} that weight is 1 and nothing changes; \code{se(x)}
alone maps \code{sigma} out at 1, leaving the familiar \code{1 / se_i^2} of a
known-variance weighted fit.

In a mixed model the residuals are conditional on the random-effect
modes, the glmmTMB convention: \code{E[Y]} is \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, not the
population-level mean.
}

\section{Residual correlation terms}{

Under an \code{ar()}, \code{ma()}, \code{arma()}, \code{cosy()} or \code{unstr()} term (see
\link{frmtmb-autocor}) the residual covariance of a group is \verb{D R D} with
\code{R} unit-diagonal and \code{D} the marginal residual SDs, and \code{"pearson"}
divides by the marginal SD of the row. That is \code{sigma} under \code{cosy()}
and \code{unstr()}. Under \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and \code{arma()} with \code{cov = TRUE},
\code{sigma} is the innovation SD, as in brms, and the marginal SD is
larger: \code{sigma / sqrt(1 - ar^2)} for an AR(1).
What \code{"response"} and \code{"pearson"} do NOT do is decorrelate: plotted
against time within a group they still show the fitted
autocorrelation, which is the intended reading. \code{"osa"} is refused,
because the taped likelihood is a joint density per group rather
than a product of per-observation terms; \code{\link[=dharma_residuals]{dharma_residuals()}} works,
since \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} draws one correlated residual per group.

\code{deviance(fit)} is unrelated: it stays \code{-2 * logLik(fit)} (the lme4
convention), which for a mixed model is the Laplace-approximated
marginal deviance and does \strong{not} equal \code{sum(residuals(fit, type = "deviance")^2)}.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(100), g = factor(rep(1:10, 10)))
dd$y <- rpois(100, exp(0.3 + 0.4 * dd$x + rnorm(10, 0, 0.6)[dd$g]))
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)) + poisson(), data = dd)

# raw and variance-standardized residuals
head(residuals(fit))
head(residuals(fit, type = "pearson"))
# the usual overdispersion check for a poisson fit
pr <- residuals(fit, type = "pearson")[, "Estimate"]
sum(pr^2) / df.residual(fit)

# one-step-ahead quantile residuals are standard normal under a
# correctly specified model, whatever the family. This block is
# \donttest{} because oneStepPredict() is 5.9 s of this example's
# 8.9 s, which puts the examples phase over R CMD check's 5 s
# threshold; --run-donttest still runs it.
\donttest{
r <- residuals(fit, type = "osa")[, "Estimate"]
qqnorm(r); qqline(r)
}
}
